  "headings": [
    {
      "tag": "H1",
      "text": "Theory Of Constraints: Using It Ahead Of Competition"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H3",
      "text": "Introducing the Theory of Constraints"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H3",
      "text": "Finding the ‘Herbie’ in your manufacturing plant"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H3",
      "text": "The constant quest for improvement"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "“As shown, once a constraint was identified and resolved, a new constraint would almost always arise (e.g. Dave).”"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H3",
      "text": "Action 1: Engage in The Theory of Constraints to ‘Eliminate’ the Constraint"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "1. Identify the system’s constraints (bottlenecks)"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "2 & 3. Exploit the system’s constraints and subordinate everything else to the decision"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "“Making the most of what you have.”"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "“Equally important, manufacturers should not rely on intuition to evaluate if the constraint has been eliminated.”"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "4. Elevate the system’s constraints"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H2",
      "text": "5. Repeat the process"
    },
    {
      "tag": "H3",
      "text": "Action 2: Making the ‘Process of Change’ mindset permanent"
